1
00:00:04,783 --> 00:00:14,490
From the urban sandlots and the wooden ballparks that first housed our national pastime, to multi-purpose concrete donuts of the 1960s and 70s, to the modern day retro

2
00:00:14,491 --> 00:00:16,466
Baseball stadiums. American icons.

3
00:00:16,467 --> 00:00:21,000
parks that find the perfect mix of baseball history and futuristic technology.

4
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:28,000
From New York's house that Ruth built to Oakland's stadium of the future.

5
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:32,000
Now, baseball parks on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Baseball, the national pastime.

7
00:00:47,001 --> 00:00:52,000
making the good times memorable and the bad times bearable.

8
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:56,000
The childhood heroes, the smell of the grass,

9
00:00:56,100 --> 00:01:00,040
the crack of the bat, and the memories of warm summer days

10
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:01,640
spent at the ballpark.

11
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:06,260
It really is something that just overwhelms you

12
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:08,760
and fills you with some sort of emotion

13
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:10,760
when you step out from underneath the stands

14
00:01:10,860 --> 00:01:12,500
and that field is before you.

15
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,960
And certainly the imagery that the ballpark embeds

16
00:01:15,961 --> 00:01:19,560
beds on you, and your mind's eye stays with you forever.

17
00:01:19,660 --> 00:01:23,160
Parks are part of the enduring legacy of baseball,

18
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:26,600
each adding its own imprint to our memories.

19
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:30,700
These ballparks, they're almost monuments to the game,

20
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:33,020
even though they're living things.

21
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:35,620
They were truly monuments to the glory

22
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:37,920
of America's national pastime.

23
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:57,160
Today's games are held in technological wonders.

24
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:04,280
The fans experiencing the game firsthand will be subjected to blinding lights, ears splitting

25
00:02:04,281 --> 00:02:10,820
noises, and fiery explosions, all of which heighten the baseball experience.

26
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:14,600
But what goes on behind the scenes is just as impressive.

27
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:20,100
The average baseball park is a city unto itself,

28
00:02:20,101 --> 00:02:25,060
able to provide food, shelter, medicine, sanitation,

29
00:02:25,061 --> 00:02:29,040
and all other necessary amenities on a massive scale.

30
00:02:29,041 --> 00:02:33,480
At a typical game, the fans will consume 26,000 hot dogs

31
00:02:33,481 --> 00:02:38,200
and sausages, 37,000 cups of soda and beer,

32
00:02:38,201 --> 00:02:41,240
tons of popcorn, peanuts, and pretzels.

33
00:02:41,241 --> 00:02:44,240
They will utilize souvenir shops, people movers,

34
00:02:44,241 --> 00:02:48,240
and 50-plus bathrooms where 40,000 gallons of water

35
00:02:48,241 --> 00:02:53,240
will flow, all services provided with assembly line efficiency

36
00:02:53,241 --> 00:02:55,380
without the fans giving it a second thought.

37
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:02,600
But unlike a city, the average park is a single structure.

38
00:03:02,601 --> 00:03:05,500
It has more than 10,000 feet of plumbing

39
00:03:05,501 --> 00:03:10,260
and enough railing to stretch five miles, over 2,000 60-foot

40
00:03:10,261 --> 00:03:14,700
pilings, anchor enough steel and concrete to build a 50-story

41
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:15,800
building.

42
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:21,200
Welcome to Camden Yards in Baltimore.

43
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:24,200
Nestled between the Baltimore docks and the historic Camden

44
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:28,820
Railroad Station, the park opened in 1992.

45
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:31,320
Camden is a thoroughly modern facility,

46
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:35,920
with the ability to seat 48,000.

47
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:39,420
Camden Yards has a beautiful facility,

48
00:03:39,421 --> 00:03:41,620
even without a baseball game going on,

49
00:03:41,621 --> 00:03:43,920
you know this is a baseball park.

50
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:46,660
This place has been built for baseball,

51
00:03:46,661 --> 00:03:50,020
and it is unlikely that anything other than baseball

52
00:03:50,021 --> 00:03:52,920
will take place in this facility.

53
00:03:52,921 --> 00:03:55,980
The secret to Camden Yard's revolutionary design

54
00:03:55,981 --> 00:03:57,580
was hindsight.

55
00:03:57,581 --> 00:04:00,760
Parks of the past were smaller than today's stadiums,

56
00:04:00,761 --> 00:04:03,280
allowing a more intimate setting.

57
00:04:03,281 --> 00:04:06,680
Games were experienced, not just watched.

58
00:04:06,681 --> 00:04:09,280
By looking to great ballparks of the past,

59
00:04:09,281 --> 00:04:12,120
designers found the key to Camden Yard's success.

60
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:17,920
The Camden Yard's really, really signaled the dawn of a

61
00:04:17,921 --> 00:04:24,520
new era in ballpark design and bringing back the unique baseball

62
00:04:24,521 --> 00:04:25,840
park experience.

63
00:04:25,841 --> 00:04:28,440
I think it's been a wonderful element added to the game, and

64
00:04:28,441 --> 00:04:32,680
I think when my successes 50 to 75 years from now look back on

65
00:04:32,681 --> 00:04:36,080
the period, they will look back on this, in this era, the same

66
00:04:36,081 --> 00:04:40,480
the same way we look back at the period 1909 to 1914,

67
00:04:40,481 --> 00:04:44,320
it's been a time that's very special.

68
00:04:44,321 --> 00:04:48,220
Baseball's story begins in the early 1800s.

69
00:04:48,221 --> 00:04:51,420
Teams representing small towns would challenge neighboring

70
00:04:51,421 --> 00:04:55,240
communities in competitions only slightly more organized

71
00:04:55,241 --> 00:04:58,340
than modern schoolyard games.

72
00:04:58,341 --> 00:05:02,140
But it was not until the 1850s that insightful businessmen

73
00:05:02,141 --> 00:05:04,340
recognized the financial potential

74
00:05:04,341 --> 00:05:07,840
and created professional teams and the novel concept of a

75
00:05:07,841 --> 00:05:11,580
ballpark where the fans would pay to see a game.

76
00:05:11,581 --> 00:05:15,320
Baseball ballparks started about the 1850s.

77
00:05:15,321 --> 00:05:18,560
It was a brand new game in the middle of the 1800s,

78
00:05:18,561 --> 00:05:22,560
and soon it became a professional game in the 1860s.

79
00:05:22,561 --> 00:05:25,520
They started building places to keep people out who didn't pay

80
00:05:25,521 --> 00:05:26,960
a little bit of money.

81
00:05:26,961 --> 00:05:32,260
It's generally regarded that the first enclosed

82
00:05:32,261 --> 00:05:38,660
ballpark was called Union Grounds, and it was in Brooklyn. It opened in 1862.

83
00:05:38,661 --> 00:05:43,540
It was a multi-purpose stadium in some senses because they also used it as an ice skating rink

84
00:05:43,541 --> 00:05:49,940
in the winter times. Union Grounds would come to typify many of the early parks.

85
00:05:49,941 --> 00:05:50,980
It was small,

86
00:05:50,981 --> 00:05:53,001
wooden, and horseshoe shaped.

87
00:05:53,002 --> 00:05:57,460
Seating only 1,500 people, it was considered large for the time.

88
00:05:57,461 --> 00:06:02,460
As the game's popularity grew, similar parks began to spring up

89
00:06:02,461 --> 00:06:05,460
around the eastern seaboard.

90
00:06:05,461 --> 00:06:08,460
So there was the National Association, then the National

91
00:06:08,461 --> 00:06:12,460
League, then the American Association, then the Union

92
00:06:12,461 --> 00:06:13,460
Association.

93
00:06:13,461 --> 00:06:17,460
So every time you have a new league, you have, depending on

94
00:06:17,461 --> 00:06:20,460
how many teams, six to 10 new parks.

95
00:06:20,461 --> 00:06:24,460
A baseball park building boom was on, but the new

96
00:06:24,461 --> 00:06:27,860
But the new ballparks faced a problem that will continue to

97
00:06:27,861 --> 00:06:31,860
plague baseball into the modern era, real estate.

98
00:06:31,861 --> 00:06:34,700
The parks had to be close enough to the cities to attract

99
00:06:34,701 --> 00:06:35,900
spectators.

100
00:06:35,901 --> 00:06:39,500
Even the most ardent fan would not travel long distances via

101
00:06:39,501 --> 00:06:42,420
horse and buggy to watch a game.

102
00:06:42,421 --> 00:06:45,920
But finding a cheap piece of land close to the city, large

103
00:06:45,921 --> 00:06:49,020
enough to hold a ballpark, was difficult.

104
00:06:49,021 --> 00:06:51,360
Baseball learned to compromise.

105
00:06:51,361 --> 00:06:54,760
Ballparks such as National League Park in Philadelphia, later

106
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:57,960
renamed the Baker Bowl, were shoehorned into the already

107
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:01,800
crowded downtown areas, giving each park its own distinctive

108
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:03,300
shape and character.

109
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:07,200
Baker Bowl started in the 1890s.

110
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:11,020
It was a ballpark where the surrounding streets determined

111
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:14,220
the size of the ballpark and determined the shape of the

112
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:15,620
ballpark.

113
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:18,860
The distance from home plate to the right field fence was

114
00:07:18,861 --> 00:07:22,600
was very short, less than 300 feet.

115
00:07:22,601 --> 00:07:26,260
The distance was so short that a 40-foot high right field

116
00:07:26,261 --> 00:07:31,100
fence had to be constructed to limit the number of home runs.

117
00:07:31,101 --> 00:07:33,400
That might be exaggerating to say the outfield wall

118
00:07:33,401 --> 00:07:35,380
cast a shadow across the infield.

119
00:07:35,381 --> 00:07:37,740
But if the right fielder had eaten onions at lunch,

120
00:07:37,741 --> 00:07:39,440
the second baseman knew it.

121
00:07:39,441 --> 00:07:42,180
Red Smith, sports writer.

122
00:07:42,181 --> 00:07:45,580
Initially, the ballparks were utilitarian, simple structures

123
00:07:45,581 --> 00:07:48,100
made of wood to seat the fans.

124
00:07:48,101 --> 00:07:53,600
But that would change with Boston's South End Grounds.

125
00:07:53,601 --> 00:07:57,040
The first real breakthrough in ballpark construction

126
00:07:57,041 --> 00:08:00,980
is when ballparks became an art, was South End Grounds in Boston.

127
00:08:00,981 --> 00:08:02,880
That was a beautiful ballpark.

128
00:08:02,881 --> 00:08:04,420
It had these witches' peaks.

129
00:08:04,421 --> 00:08:06,060
They're just for decoration.

130
00:08:06,061 --> 00:08:07,820
And that's the first time that owners really

131
00:08:07,821 --> 00:08:09,760
spent on decorating their parks.

132
00:08:09,761 --> 00:08:12,860
Like a scene from Camelot, the South End Grounds

133
00:08:12,861 --> 00:08:15,900
looked more like a place where armored knights rather than

134
00:08:15,901 --> 00:08:21,900
players would do battle. Initially built in 1871, by 1877,

135
00:08:21,901 --> 00:08:25,500
after continual renovation, the two-tiered grandstands and

136
00:08:25,501 --> 00:08:29,740
adjoining bleachers had a seating capacity of 5,000.

137
00:08:29,741 --> 00:08:32,860
When you see that park with those witches' peaks, those

138
00:08:32,861 --> 00:08:37,660
conical tops on it, you know that this is not just another

139
00:08:37,661 --> 00:08:42,460
wooden slap-it-up-in-a-couple-of-weeks kind of a job.

140
00:08:42,461 --> 00:08:46,860
This was clearly meant to present the ballpark

141
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:49,000
as an entertainment palace.

142
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:51,800
Crowds continued to grow, and the parks

143
00:08:51,900 --> 00:08:55,000
were regularly altered to seat more fans.

144
00:08:55,100 --> 00:08:56,700
Some of the remedies to overcrowding

145
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:59,220
would be unheard of today.

146
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:01,520
In those early days, the crowd was allowed in the field.

147
00:09:01,620 --> 00:09:03,920
There was not that much attention to pay to the home run,

148
00:09:04,020 --> 00:09:06,920
so people were allowed to stand at the back of the park.

149
00:09:07,020 --> 00:09:09,020
They would often rope off part of the outfield

150
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:11,120
and let the fans stand there.

151
00:09:11,121 --> 00:09:14,120
And so then you had a problem of what to do with your ground

152
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:16,520
rules when a ball would be hit into these fans,

153
00:09:16,620 --> 00:09:20,120
it would ordinarily just be an open field of play.

154
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:22,460
The Polo Grounds at Coogan's Bluff,

155
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:24,960
located along the Harlem River in Manhattan,

156
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:27,660
was constructed in 1891.

157
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:31,780
Originally seating 5,000 fans, through many incarnations,

158
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:35,580
the park was eventually able to seat 16,000 fans by the end

159
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:37,280
of the 19th century.

160
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:40,320
Growing popularity and larger, more opulent facilities

161
00:09:40,321 --> 00:09:42,720
facilities were a boon to baseball.

162
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:45,920
But these outward successes masked a menacing, sometimes

163
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:49,560
deadly, threat, fire.

164
00:09:49,660 --> 00:09:52,660
Obviously, the earliest ballparks were made of wood.

165
00:09:52,760 --> 00:09:54,760
And because of that, they didn't last too long.

166
00:09:54,860 --> 00:09:59,080
Fires were a common problem with these ballparks.

167
00:09:59,180 --> 00:10:02,340
In fact, the original white stocking grounds in Chicago

168
00:10:02,440 --> 00:10:04,580
burned down in the great fire, you

169
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:08,720
know, the one that was started by Mrs. O'Leary's cow, in 1871.

170
00:10:08,721 --> 00:10:12,680
People smoking cigarettes or cigars carelessly drop it on the seats.

171
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:15,740
These parts would catch on fire. Huge sections would be destroyed.

172
00:10:15,860 --> 00:10:18,740
They'd have to either rebuild or start over from scratch.

173
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:22,100
Most of the fires were started accidentally by the fans

174
00:10:22,101 --> 00:10:25,400
with carelessly discarded cigars or smoldering matches.

175
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:30,340
The 1890s saw at least 21 ballparks burst into flames.

176
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:35,580
During one particularly bad season, 20 people lost their lives.

177
00:10:35,581 --> 00:10:38,220
If baseball were to continue to grow,

178
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:42,220
the parks would need to be both larger and safer.

179
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:45,320
It would take a leap forward in construction technology

180
00:10:45,420 --> 00:10:50,320
to propel baseball into its golden age.

181
00:10:50,420 --> 00:10:54,240
From 1887 to 1920, the Baker Bowl's field

182
00:10:54,340 --> 00:10:57,840
was cut not by a lawnmower, but by three sheep.

183
00:11:02,040 --> 00:11:04,540
By the turn of the century, baseball

184
00:11:04,541 --> 00:11:06,273
baseball was well established.

185
00:11:06,274 --> 00:11:09,740
Thousands flocked to parks up and down the eastern seaboard.

186
00:11:10,380 --> 00:11:14,060
The simple wooden structures couldn't contain the game's growing popularity.

187
00:11:15,980 --> 00:11:18,111
Fire was not the only hazard.

188
00:11:18,112 --> 00:11:23,180
In August of 1903, the stands in Philadelphia's baker bowl collapsed,

189
00:11:23,181 --> 00:11:28,140
killing 12 and injuring 100. That's one of the dark sides of

190
00:11:28,141 --> 00:11:33,240
ballparks in general is fans who are either seriously injured

191
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:37,980
or die falling out of stands or having stands collapse.

192
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:43,080
So it got to be a serious, serious endeavor then.

193
00:11:43,180 --> 00:11:45,200
Anything that's concerned with human life and money

194
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:47,900
is generally serious.

195
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:51,800
Baseball had outgrown its quaint old wooden ballparks.

196
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:54,040
The next generation of parks would follow the lead

197
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:57,340
of other industries and take advantage of relatively new

198
00:11:57,341 --> 00:12:02,080
construction materials. Pennsylvania, home to America's

199
00:12:02,180 --> 00:12:05,440
booming steel industry, would be at the forefront.

200
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:08,880
Games would now be played in stadiums built from steel and

201
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:12,180
reinforced concrete. The thing about steel and

202
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:17,700
concrete was that you could take this and round it behind home

203
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:23,300
plate gradually to get all kinds of wonderful seats behind home

204
00:12:23,301 --> 00:12:27,900
And with concrete and steel, of course,

205
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,400
came the possibility of double-decking the park.

206
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:33,040
And of course, you could extend the wings

207
00:12:33,140 --> 00:12:35,040
up the left and right field lines.

208
00:12:35,140 --> 00:12:37,940
And that was the real genius of it.

209
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:39,540
So it created this grand environment

210
00:12:39,640 --> 00:12:41,460
in which people would come to the game

211
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:43,960
to watch their favorite team.

212
00:12:44,060 --> 00:12:47,560
Philadelphia's Scheib Park, named after team owner

213
00:12:47,660 --> 00:12:51,460
Ben Scheib, became the first steel and concrete stadium

214
00:12:51,461 --> 00:12:54,800
capable of seating 30,000 fans.

215
00:12:54,900 --> 00:12:57,460
This park retained its intimate surroundings,

216
00:12:57,560 --> 00:13:00,300
but also boasted such improvements as ramps,

217
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:03,200
rooms for umpires and visiting teams,

218
00:13:03,300 --> 00:13:07,600
and a copula, the precursor to the modern skybox.

219
00:13:07,700 --> 00:13:10,120
Constructed in the French Renaissance style,

220
00:13:10,220 --> 00:13:12,720
the park featured decorative concrete trim

221
00:13:12,820 --> 00:13:16,420
over every archway and window.

222
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:18,860
It didn't look like just an ordinary place

223
00:13:18,960 --> 00:13:20,420
where baseball games were played.

224
00:13:20,421 --> 00:13:28,820
were played it looked like places were almost for a religious ceremony that's because around 1910

225
00:13:28,821 --> 00:13:36,980
baseball was peaking in terms of its popularity its popularity began really accelerating around

226
00:13:36,981 --> 00:13:45,700
1908 to 1910 19 teens were baseball was the national game without question

227
00:13:45,701 --> 00:13:50,080
And Scheib Park reflected this.

228
00:13:50,081 --> 00:13:53,100
It was a temple to this new sport that had taken over

229
00:13:53,101 --> 00:13:54,800
the country.

230
00:13:54,801 --> 00:13:57,140
Scheib's original right field fence

231
00:13:57,141 --> 00:14:01,120
stood 12 feet tall, allowing enterprising residents who

232
00:14:01,121 --> 00:14:03,960
lived on the adjoining street to watch home games

233
00:14:03,961 --> 00:14:07,120
from the comfort of their own roofs.

234
00:14:07,121 --> 00:14:10,420
Some even built bleachers, which began to cut into ballpark

235
00:14:10,421 --> 00:14:12,000
revenue.

236
00:14:12,001 --> 00:14:16,900
The solution came in 1935 in the form of a 22-foot vertical

237
00:14:16,901 --> 00:14:20,960
extension to the existing wall, a wall locals continue to refer

238
00:14:20,961 --> 00:14:24,780
to as the Spite Fence.

239
00:14:24,781 --> 00:14:27,280
Carl Scheib and his partner, Connie Mack,

240
00:14:27,281 --> 00:14:30,860
put together the first original steel and concrete park,

241
00:14:30,861 --> 00:14:33,560
which opened in 1909.

242
00:14:33,561 --> 00:14:40,100
And that is really the first, quote, modern ballpark.

243
00:14:40,101 --> 00:14:42,800
I should also add that it came after 1908,

244
00:14:42,840 --> 00:14:45,140
the year that Take Me Out to the Ball Game was written,

245
00:14:45,180 --> 00:14:49,000
also the year that the first real, wonderful baseball cards

246
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:51,940
were printed by American Tobacco.

247
00:14:51,980 --> 00:14:53,640
So that was a big year.

248
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:56,080
And with baseball on the front page in eight cities,

249
00:14:56,120 --> 00:14:59,620
you can see that everybody and his brother was saying,

250
00:14:59,660 --> 00:15:03,260
oh boy, oh boy, now we're gonna build a big ballpark.

251
00:15:03,300 --> 00:15:08,200
And that really is the story from 1909 to 1915.

252
00:15:08,201 --> 00:15:12,680
The next five years were a golden age for ballpark construction.

253
00:15:13,540 --> 00:15:17,220
No fewer than 11 parks with legendary names such as Wrigley

254
00:15:17,221 --> 00:15:19,720
and Fenway

255
00:15:19,721 --> 00:15:25,260
would rise as shrines to America's love for the game.

256
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:28,640
Comiskey Park in Chicago,

257
00:15:29,320 --> 00:15:31,680
which was the home of the White Sox for 80 seasons,

258
00:15:32,320 --> 00:15:35,800
Crosley Field in Cincinnati was another of those classic jewel boxes,

259
00:15:35,801 --> 00:15:36,680
as they were called,

260
00:15:36,681 --> 00:15:41,620
because they were intimate and they had this real character, this real grandeur to them.

261
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:47,840
When you talk about the urban ballparks like Ebbets Field, Forbes Field, Fenway Park,

262
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:52,040
you're talking about ballparks that were right in a neighborhood.

263
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:57,960
And so it would stand to reason that there would be more of a community feeling.

264
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:04,080
The most unique thing about all those parks to me is the fact that they were fit on an urban parcel.

265
00:16:06,680 --> 00:16:10,220
which made for some unique angles.

266
00:16:10,320 --> 00:16:14,580
And the fact that they tended to go straight up on the sides

267
00:16:14,680 --> 00:16:18,620
so that instead of going back and pushing the upper deck

268
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:20,920
farther away from the playing field,

269
00:16:21,020 --> 00:16:24,340
you had an upper deck in addition to a lower deck that

270
00:16:24,440 --> 00:16:26,840
was very close to the field and ultimately a more

271
00:16:26,940 --> 00:16:29,640
intimate experience.

272
00:16:29,740 --> 00:16:32,140
Other landmark ballparks owe their existence

273
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:33,540
to more than just baseball.

274
00:16:36,680 --> 00:16:38,820
Fenway Park.

275
00:16:38,821 --> 00:16:41,820
If you know what a fen is, it's a bog.

276
00:16:41,821 --> 00:16:45,220
And it was owned by the family that owned the Boston Globe,

277
00:16:45,221 --> 00:16:48,220
and it was a real estate scam, in essence.

278
00:16:48,221 --> 00:16:54,140
They couldn't move this land, so Junior Taylor decided to buy

279
00:16:54,141 --> 00:16:57,240
the Red Sox, build them a ballpark, sell it at a profit,

280
00:16:57,241 --> 00:17:00,140
and get out, which is exactly what happened.

281
00:17:00,141 --> 00:17:02,140
Though less than ideally located,

282
00:17:02,141 --> 00:17:04,740
the parks utilized state-of-the-art construction

283
00:17:04,741 --> 00:17:06,040
techniques.

284
00:17:06,041 --> 00:17:09,880
Structural steel and reinforced concrete allowed the parks to be

285
00:17:09,881 --> 00:17:13,480
stronger and, to the owner's delight, larger.

286
00:17:13,481 --> 00:17:17,880
They were more easily erected, durable, and had the added bonus

287
00:17:17,881 --> 00:17:22,080
of being fireproof, qualities necessary for the next step in

288
00:17:22,081 --> 00:17:25,700
ballpark evolution, the super stadium.

289
00:17:25,701 --> 00:17:29,500
Introduced into ballparks in 1905, the red hot dachshund

290
00:17:29,501 --> 00:17:33,400
sausage on a roll had its name shortened to hot dog by a

291
00:17:33,401 --> 00:17:37,300
by a newspaper cartoonist who could not spell the word dachshund.

292
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:43,640
With baseball's growing popularity,

293
00:17:43,641 --> 00:17:46,440
even larger stadiums were needed.

294
00:17:46,441 --> 00:17:48,780
Existing parks were regularly altered

295
00:17:48,781 --> 00:17:50,820
to increase their seating capacity.

296
00:17:50,821 --> 00:17:53,680
But building additional seats into the already shoehorned

297
00:17:53,681 --> 00:17:59,000
parks made for some interesting configurations.

298
00:17:59,001 --> 00:18:00,900
A number of the traditional, the older ballpacks

299
00:18:00,901 --> 00:18:02,900
had some idiosyncrasies.

300
00:18:02,901 --> 00:18:05,600
The polo grounds, the clubhouse was in center field,

301
00:18:05,601 --> 00:18:07,640
so after the game the players from both teams

302
00:18:07,641 --> 00:18:11,340
had to run out and scoot across the field up the stairs

303
00:18:11,341 --> 00:18:14,680
behind the center field's grandstand.

304
00:18:14,681 --> 00:18:17,520
Fenway Park left field had a hill.

305
00:18:17,521 --> 00:18:21,060
Also, I believe Crosley Field did as well the same thing.

306
00:18:21,061 --> 00:18:26,520
They had a berm that actually ran up to the outfield fence.

307
00:18:26,521 --> 00:18:28,660
Sometimes there were owners of houses

308
00:18:28,661 --> 00:18:30,420
that refused to sell their property,

309
00:18:30,421 --> 00:18:32,560
and a classic example of this is Griffith Stadium

310
00:18:32,561 --> 00:18:37,060
in Washington, D.C., if you look at an aerial view of the ballpark, you'll see that the

311
00:18:37,061 --> 00:18:41,440
outfield stands come to a point and then point inwards right in center field, and just on

312
00:18:41,441 --> 00:18:46,060
the other side of the fence, there is a house there, and that person refused to sell, so

313
00:18:46,061 --> 00:18:49,740
they had to build a ballpark literally around that house.

314
00:18:49,741 --> 00:18:51,657
Park owners had to be careful.

315
00:18:51,658 --> 00:18:55,620
The idiosyncrasies which gave the parks their unique feel were

316
00:18:55,621 --> 00:19:00,320
a windfall to crafty fans. A lot of people watched the ballgames

317
00:19:00,321 --> 00:19:06,400
for free at the polar grounds from Coogan's Bluff, which was

318
00:19:06,401 --> 00:19:13,760
a high rise of a hill that went well above the top of the stadium

319
00:19:13,761 --> 00:19:16,240
so that you were behind home plate.

320
00:19:16,241 --> 00:19:20,140
And if you look down, you see the roof of the ballpark.

321
00:19:20,141 --> 00:19:23,980
The revamping of old parks could only go so far.

322
00:19:23,981 --> 00:19:26,940
New construction technology allowed the first of a number

323
00:19:26,941 --> 00:19:29,980
of super stadiums to be built.

324
00:19:29,981 --> 00:19:34,060
Constructed in 1915, Braves Field in Boston

325
00:19:34,160 --> 00:19:36,920
was the world's largest ballpark to date.

326
00:19:37,020 --> 00:19:40,260
Costing over a million dollars and equal in size

327
00:19:40,360 --> 00:19:46,660
to an 18-hole golf course, the stadium sat 42,000.

328
00:19:46,760 --> 00:19:49,900
The brainchild of Braves owner James Gaffney,

329
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:53,600
the park was designed to create his favorite type of plays.

330
00:19:53,700 --> 00:19:57,440
Inside the park, home runs with a distance of 402 feet

331
00:19:57,441 --> 00:20:04,020
down each foul line and an immense 550 feet to center field, not one home run cleared the

332
00:20:04,021 --> 00:20:08,160
fences in Braves Field during its first 10 years in use.

333
00:20:08,161 --> 00:20:15,520
That was before the home run became very popular, and baseball at that time was a baseball

334
00:20:15,521 --> 00:20:21,500
of one-run games, guys getting on first, trying to steal second, hit and run, trying

335
00:20:21,501 --> 00:20:26,100
one run at a time, and Bravesfield was built on the basis

336
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:27,900
that this is the way you played baseball.

337
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:29,740
You didn't try to hit home runs.

338
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:33,040
You tried to get doubles and triples and steal bases and so

339
00:20:33,140 --> 00:20:34,340
on.

340
00:20:34,440 --> 00:20:38,080
Then, when you tried to steal second and the ball went past

341
00:20:38,180 --> 00:20:40,420
the second baseman into the outfield, you might get all the

342
00:20:40,520 --> 00:20:43,560
way home before the outfielder could get to it.

343
00:20:43,660 --> 00:20:45,760
In contrast, an even larger stadium was being

344
00:20:45,860 --> 00:20:48,820
constructed that would become famous for the number of home

345
00:20:48,821 --> 00:20:51,720
hit, as well as the men who hit them.

346
00:20:51,820 --> 00:20:56,060
A new era began in 1922, when the Yankees made their move

347
00:20:56,160 --> 00:20:59,060
from the 30,000 seat polo grounds, which they shared

348
00:20:59,160 --> 00:21:05,060
with the New York Giants, to the 75,000 seat Yankee Stadium.

349
00:21:05,160 --> 00:21:08,480
What would seem to be a natural move for the Yankees was more

350
00:21:08,580 --> 00:21:11,480
an exercise in survival.

351
00:21:11,580 --> 00:21:15,980
In 1921, a jealous John McGraw, owner of the polo grounds

352
00:21:15,981 --> 00:21:18,980
and rival New York Giants, evicted the Yankees

353
00:21:18,981 --> 00:21:21,980
from their uptown Manhattan location.

354
00:21:21,981 --> 00:21:24,980
McGraw was unable to contend with the Yankees' growing gate

355
00:21:24,981 --> 00:21:28,980
receipts and popularity of their star slugger,

356
00:21:28,981 --> 00:21:30,980
George Herman Ruth.

357
00:21:30,981 --> 00:21:33,980
McGraw's plan for the Yankees was simple.

358
00:21:33,981 --> 00:21:37,980
If we kick them out, they won't be able to find another location

359
00:21:37,981 --> 00:21:38,980
on Manhattan Island.

360
00:21:38,981 --> 00:21:41,980
They'll have to move to the Bronx or Long Island.

361
00:21:41,981 --> 00:21:43,980
The fans will forget all about them,

362
00:21:43,981 --> 00:21:46,480
all about them, and they'll be through.

363
00:21:46,580 --> 00:21:48,980
The Yankees fought back by constructing

364
00:21:49,080 --> 00:21:52,320
the greatest baseball stadium ever seen,

365
00:21:52,420 --> 00:21:55,520
the location chosen just across the Harlem River

366
00:21:55,620 --> 00:21:58,420
within eyesight of the polo grounds.

367
00:21:58,520 --> 00:22:01,440
The Yankees stadium was a pinnacle

368
00:22:01,540 --> 00:22:05,240
and maybe the pinnacle for ballpark construction.

369
00:22:05,340 --> 00:22:08,540
It was the first triple-decker stadium.

370
00:22:08,640 --> 00:22:13,980
It was the first huge stadium, 65,000 and change

371
00:22:13,981 --> 00:22:20,980
70,000, 78,000 for some games, even though the fire marshals were petrified.

372
00:22:20,981 --> 00:22:24,980
And it was all made possible by Babe Ruth.

373
00:22:24,981 --> 00:22:27,980
So it's called the house that Ruth built.

374
00:22:35,980 --> 00:22:40,980
Ah, Homer, the first in Yankee Stadium, the first of many for Babe Ruth.

375
00:22:40,981 --> 00:22:43,780
1923 is a great year for Rude.

376
00:22:43,880 --> 00:22:45,780
He sets his all-time batting mark,

377
00:22:45,880 --> 00:22:48,280
and the Yanks win their first World Series.

378
00:22:48,380 --> 00:22:50,720
Though credited to the Sultan of Swat,

379
00:22:50,820 --> 00:22:53,020
the true builders of the stadium were the team

380
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:56,220
of New York-based White Construction Company

381
00:22:56,320 --> 00:22:59,140
and Cleveland's Osborne Engineering.

382
00:22:59,240 --> 00:23:02,140
Osborne's prior baseball park experience

383
00:23:02,240 --> 00:23:05,140
had made it the logical choice for the job.

384
00:23:05,240 --> 00:23:10,980
Osborne Engineering Company, from the turn of the start,

385
00:23:10,981 --> 00:23:16,180
century, starting with a polar ground in New York,

386
00:23:16,181 --> 00:23:19,860
designed the Forbes Field, Eberts Field, Fenway Park,

387
00:23:19,861 --> 00:23:21,220
Yankee Stadium.

388
00:23:21,221 --> 00:23:25,820
We did over 100 stadiums over the years.

389
00:23:25,821 --> 00:23:26,900
Osborne did the plans.

390
00:23:26,901 --> 00:23:29,500
White Construction did the construction.

391
00:23:29,501 --> 00:23:30,800
Promised to have it done in a year.

392
00:23:30,801 --> 00:23:32,600
Did it in 11 months.

393
00:23:32,601 --> 00:23:38,500
Under budget, just about the smoothest job going.

394
00:23:38,501 --> 00:23:40,740
And the numbers are staggering, the amount of wood,

395
00:23:40,741 --> 00:23:43,080
the amount of concrete, the amount of steel.

396
00:23:43,180 --> 00:23:48,040
2,200 tons of structural steel and 28,000 cubic yards

397
00:23:48,140 --> 00:23:51,720
of reinforced concrete were needed to construct the stadium,

398
00:23:51,820 --> 00:23:54,820
which occupied 10 acres of land.

399
00:23:54,920 --> 00:23:57,820
An additional 950,000 board feet of lumber

400
00:23:57,920 --> 00:24:01,560
were used to construct the bleachers.

401
00:24:01,660 --> 00:24:04,400
The $2 million facility was the first venue

402
00:24:04,500 --> 00:24:09,560
to be called a stadium, where fans watched men with names

403
00:24:09,561 --> 00:24:12,960
like DiMaggio, Gehrig, and Luth.

404
00:24:20,300 --> 00:24:23,500
The stadiums and super stadiums constructed in the first quarter

405
00:24:23,501 --> 00:24:27,880
of the century would serve baseball for decades.

406
00:24:27,881 --> 00:24:33,880
From 1923 to 1952, only one baseball stadium was built,

407
00:24:33,881 --> 00:24:36,520
Cleveland's Municipal Stadium.

408
00:24:36,521 --> 00:24:39,160
But baseball itself did not stagnate.

409
00:24:39,161 --> 00:24:44,080
In 1935, one technical achievement would forever change the way we watched the game.

410
00:24:45,460 --> 00:24:46,160
Lights.

411
00:24:47,340 --> 00:24:53,140
The first night game was played at Crossley Field in Cincinnati, May 24, 1935.

412
00:24:54,040 --> 00:24:58,040
And President Roosevelt threw the switch in the White House, activating the lights.

413
00:24:58,920 --> 00:25:01,780
Night baseball really revolutionized the sport.

414
00:25:02,320 --> 00:25:08,280
As team owner after team owner realized the riches that could be enjoyed by playing the baseball games

415
00:25:08,281 --> 00:25:13,080
under the lights so that working class people could attend games in the evenings instead

416
00:25:13,081 --> 00:25:15,280
of missing them during the day when they would be at work.

417
00:25:15,281 --> 00:25:21,900
Wrigley Field, of course, was the famous holdout of putting lights in Wrigley Field, and they

418
00:25:21,901 --> 00:25:23,880
eventually did in 1988.

419
00:25:23,881 --> 00:25:28,400
But the story behind that is they were all set to bring in lights for night baseball

420
00:25:28,401 --> 00:25:29,400
in 1941.

421
00:25:29,401 --> 00:25:34,000
After the 1941 baseball season, they ordered all the supplies, all the parts to bring lights,

422
00:25:34,001 --> 00:25:35,560
install them.

423
00:25:35,561 --> 00:25:38,200
And then the Japanese attacked Pearl Harbor, the United States

424
00:25:38,300 --> 00:25:42,060
was thrown into war, and the team donated all the supplies

425
00:25:42,160 --> 00:25:45,360
and all the materials to the war effort.

426
00:25:45,460 --> 00:25:47,840
With the light came larger crowds,

427
00:25:47,940 --> 00:25:51,500
and the old stadiums needed to make way for the new.

428
00:25:51,600 --> 00:25:55,520
An era ended in 1960 with the raising of Ebbets Field

429
00:25:55,620 --> 00:25:56,420
in Brooklyn.

430
00:25:56,520 --> 00:25:59,520
And now it's play ball again, but not the sort

431
00:25:59,620 --> 00:26:00,860
Dodger fans cheer.

432
00:26:05,560 --> 00:26:10,560
This time, Ebbets Field itself is struck out.

433
00:26:10,561 --> 00:26:16,560
As the venues aged, the cost of operations and upkeep increased.

434
00:26:16,561 --> 00:26:21,560
Due to economic difficulties, by the 1960s, team owners and city

435
00:26:21,561 --> 00:26:25,560
officials thought it best to create one home for all

436
00:26:25,561 --> 00:26:28,560
professional sporting activities, thus spreading the

437
00:26:28,561 --> 00:26:31,560
financial burden. The site of Baltimore's Oriel Park

438
00:26:31,561 --> 00:26:34,560
at Camden Yards is only two blocks from Babe Ruth's birthplace.

439
00:26:34,561 --> 00:26:35,573
Ruth's birthplace.

440
00:26:35,574 --> 00:26:39,680
In fact, his father's saloon, Ruth's Cafe, was located on what is now the

441
00:26:39,681 --> 00:26:41,520
Park Center field.

442
00:26:41,521 --> 00:26:49,800
By the 1960s, parks built in the first quarter of the century were deteriorating.

443
00:26:50,800 --> 00:26:54,175
The stadiums were becoming more and more expensive to maintain.

444
00:26:54,176 --> 00:26:55,300
Always on the lookout

445
00:26:55,301 --> 00:27:01,360
for a way to save money, team owners joined local politicians and decided the new stadiums

446
00:27:01,361 --> 00:27:08,160
to be multi-purpose facilities. You have ball clubs that are

447
00:27:08,161 --> 00:27:13,200
saying, now we're in ballparks that are pushing 60 years old.

448
00:27:13,201 --> 00:27:18,000
They're starting to show their age. Our people want a nicer

449
00:27:18,001 --> 00:27:22,320
ambiance to go to the ballpark. To combat ever-rising real

450
00:27:22,321 --> 00:27:26,160
estate prices, for the first time, owners looked outside of

451
00:27:26,161 --> 00:27:30,160
city limits for places to build their new, modern stadiums.

452
00:27:30,161 --> 00:27:32,600
The stadiums that were built, that started to be built in

453
00:27:32,700 --> 00:27:37,260
the 50s and the 60s and the 70s tended to be located outside

454
00:27:37,360 --> 00:27:41,840
of cities on large plots of land that planners call

455
00:27:41,940 --> 00:27:44,840
super blocks, which meant that the stadium was not

456
00:27:44,940 --> 00:27:46,380
constrained by anything.

457
00:27:46,480 --> 00:27:48,540
You had the stadium located on the super block surrounded

458
00:27:48,640 --> 00:27:50,040
by parking.

459
00:27:50,140 --> 00:27:52,620
And typically sites were chosen for their proximity

460
00:27:52,720 --> 00:27:54,320
to freeways.

461
00:27:54,420 --> 00:27:57,920
The new park sacrificed the ambiance and intimacy

462
00:27:58,020 --> 00:27:59,820
of the older parks.

463
00:27:59,821 --> 00:28:02,220
One of the most otherworldly-looking stadiums

464
00:28:02,320 --> 00:28:05,220
was Houston's Astrodome, dubbed the eighth wonder

465
00:28:05,320 --> 00:28:06,420
of the world.

466
00:28:06,520 --> 00:28:11,360
Construction on the 74,000-seat stadium began in 1962.

467
00:28:11,460 --> 00:28:14,360
At a cost of $35 million, the Astrodome

468
00:28:14,460 --> 00:28:16,880
ushered in a new era of baseball parks.

469
00:28:16,980 --> 00:28:20,280
Its builders created an 18-story high dome ceiling

470
00:28:20,380 --> 00:28:23,280
that could house baseball, football, basketball,

471
00:28:23,380 --> 00:28:25,520
and countless other events.

472
00:28:25,620 --> 00:28:28,280
Like many of the newer stadiums, the Astrodome

473
00:28:28,281 --> 00:28:31,780
seemed to suffer from an identity crisis.

474
00:28:31,880 --> 00:28:35,120
The new breed of stadiums were built to save money,

475
00:28:35,220 --> 00:28:37,720
but they were not without their luxuries.

476
00:28:37,820 --> 00:28:41,220
The Astrodome introduced the very first skybox.

477
00:28:41,320 --> 00:28:45,460
The exclusive skyboxes, a place for relaxation

478
00:28:45,560 --> 00:28:47,240
and entertainment.

479
00:28:47,340 --> 00:28:51,400
Individually decorated suites with built-in closed circuit

480
00:28:51,500 --> 00:28:52,740
TV.

481
00:28:52,840 --> 00:28:55,380
This is really some kind of baseball.

482
00:28:55,381 --> 00:29:00,680
The success of the Astrodome Skybox created the desire for

483
00:29:00,780 --> 00:29:03,480
skyboxes in most of the parks to follow.

484
00:29:03,580 --> 00:29:07,120
The skyboxes represent the ultimate in creature comforts.

485
00:29:07,220 --> 00:29:11,160
They became a huge success, but not all innovations were so

486
00:29:11,260 --> 00:29:14,100
well received.

487
00:29:14,200 --> 00:29:17,040
When the old ballparks started to crumble, they were replaced

488
00:29:17,140 --> 00:29:20,600
with multi-purpose stadiums, in which both baseball and football

489
00:29:20,700 --> 00:29:22,400
were played.

490
00:29:22,401 --> 00:29:29,400
Unfortunately for baseball, the spectator demands between the two sports were very different.

491
00:29:29,401 --> 00:29:36,880
Which is easy enough to visualize once you think about the playing fields of the two

492
00:29:36,881 --> 00:29:37,581
sports.

493
00:29:37,582 --> 00:29:43,720
Huge majority of the seats are nowhere near the infield, and therefore it took away

494
00:29:43,721 --> 00:29:47,840
the intimacy of the game. Another complaint about the Astrodome

495
00:29:47,841 --> 00:29:52,520
involved the field itself. Its evolution was due to an unforeseen

496
00:29:52,521 --> 00:29:56,380
chain of events. Originally, the dome's translucent ceiling

497
00:29:56,381 --> 00:30:00,660
panels created so much glare that players were blinded when they looked up.

498
00:30:01,740 --> 00:30:05,560
To reduce the glare, an acrylic coating was applied to the panels.

499
00:30:06,340 --> 00:30:10,140
The glare was reduced, but the lack of light killed the grass.

500
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:14,960
The sod was removed, and a plastic grass carpet was installed.

501
00:30:14,961 --> 00:30:18,960
The synthetic surface was dubbed astroturf,

502
00:30:19,060 --> 00:30:21,960
and many players claimed it was like playing baseball

503
00:30:22,060 --> 00:30:23,960
on concrete.

504
00:30:24,060 --> 00:30:27,000
For purists, this mix of baseball and technology

505
00:30:27,100 --> 00:30:28,900
was near sacrilege.

506
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:33,420
But there was no refuting that this was the wave of the future.

507
00:30:33,520 --> 00:30:35,920
The Astrodome featured the world's first exploding

508
00:30:36,020 --> 00:30:37,020
scoreboard.

509
00:30:37,120 --> 00:30:39,960
More than half an acre of computer sequenced lights,

510
00:30:40,060 --> 00:30:43,920
14,000 lights in all, would raise the ballpark in the world

511
00:30:43,921 --> 00:30:47,820
the ballpark experience to a new level.

512
00:30:47,920 --> 00:30:49,320
It's a home run!

513
00:30:57,760 --> 00:31:00,380
The late 1960s brought about cookie-cutter,

514
00:31:00,480 --> 00:31:02,380
multi-purpose facilities.

515
00:31:02,480 --> 00:31:06,380
With names like Riverfront Stadium, Three Rivers Stadium,

516
00:31:06,480 --> 00:31:10,520
and Veterans Stadium, they each seated 50,000 fans

517
00:31:10,620 --> 00:31:12,680
and had symmetrical playing surfaces

518
00:31:12,681 --> 00:31:15,420
covered with astroturf.

519
00:31:15,520 --> 00:31:17,880
As widespread as the concrete donuts became,

520
00:31:17,980 --> 00:31:21,860
they were never accepted by the fans, at least the baseball fans,

521
00:31:21,960 --> 00:31:24,160
and were always looked upon with derision.

522
00:31:24,260 --> 00:31:26,760
I spent nine years living in Philadelphia.

523
00:31:26,860 --> 00:31:28,860
When I arrived in Philadelphia in the early 70s,

524
00:31:28,960 --> 00:31:35,400
the skeleton of Scheib Park was still there, obviously desolate

525
00:31:35,500 --> 00:31:37,800
and ready to be torn down.

526
00:31:37,900 --> 00:31:41,000
And the vet at that time was about three years old.

527
00:31:41,001 --> 00:31:43,600
The fans weren't the only ones unhappy with baseball's

528
00:31:43,700 --> 00:31:46,600
concrete donuts.

529
00:31:46,700 --> 00:31:48,640
Richie Hebner of the Pirates always said that,

530
00:31:48,740 --> 00:31:50,640
that there were some days he'd be out in the field

531
00:31:50,740 --> 00:31:52,740
and forget where he was because he'd look around the park

532
00:31:52,840 --> 00:31:55,540
and he wouldn't be able to tell which stadium he was in.

533
00:31:55,640 --> 00:31:56,940
At the time, they were fresh.

534
00:31:57,040 --> 00:31:57,960
They were new.

535
00:31:58,060 --> 00:32:01,060
They seemed almost modern, state of the art.

536
00:32:01,160 --> 00:32:03,960
Then over, gradually over time, people

537
00:32:04,060 --> 00:32:06,500
started to recognize the lack of character.

538
00:32:06,600 --> 00:32:09,260
The casualties of these new modern multipurpose

539
00:32:09,261 --> 00:32:14,160
facilities were the quaint, old, downtown, historic ballparks.

540
00:32:14,260 --> 00:32:18,260
One by one, teams left parks from the golden era of ballpark

541
00:32:18,360 --> 00:32:23,400
construction, which were not only replaced, but demolished.

542
00:32:23,500 --> 00:32:28,020
Cincinnati's Crossley Field, built in 1912, was raised in

543
00:32:28,120 --> 00:32:29,120
1970.

544
00:32:32,520 --> 00:32:35,620
Pittsburgh's Forbes Field, the second stadium built out of

545
00:32:35,720 --> 00:32:39,260
steel and concrete, was demolished in 1971.

546
00:32:39,261 --> 00:32:46,300
And Philadelphia's Shive Park, the very first concrete and steel park, was demolished in 1976.

547
00:32:47,900 --> 00:32:51,420
The 1960s were the dark ages of ballpark construction.

548
00:32:52,280 --> 00:32:56,600
Our nation was tired of chaos, and the old ballparks were kind of chaotic.

549
00:32:56,800 --> 00:32:58,200
They had irregular fences.

550
00:32:58,740 --> 00:33:00,100
You never knew what to expect.

551
00:33:00,300 --> 00:33:02,740
There were funny bounces on the outfield walls.

552
00:33:02,800 --> 00:33:04,240
There were funny bounces on the grass.

553
00:33:04,720 --> 00:33:08,520
And the whole nation just decided that uniformity was beautiful.

554
00:33:08,521 --> 00:33:11,360
and you can understand by what was going on in our society.

555
00:33:11,460 --> 00:33:13,660
However, it was the dark ages of ballpark constructions

556
00:33:13,760 --> 00:33:15,360
because they were all the same.

557
00:33:15,460 --> 00:33:19,700
You saw a tendency for the new baseball and sports facilities

558
00:33:19,800 --> 00:33:21,460
to be placed in the outskirts of town,

559
00:33:21,560 --> 00:33:25,040
like on a beltway with huge parking lots surrounding them.

560
00:33:25,140 --> 00:33:26,500
Since most of the fans at that point

561
00:33:26,600 --> 00:33:29,000
were coming by car and not by mass transit,

562
00:33:29,100 --> 00:33:30,680
they wanted to make it easy for the fans

563
00:33:30,780 --> 00:33:33,380
to get to the stadium and get back on the highway

564
00:33:33,480 --> 00:33:34,620
and go home afterwards.

565
00:33:34,720 --> 00:33:36,720
The building trend continued.

566
00:33:36,721 --> 00:33:42,820
From 1962 to 1989, just one baseball-only stadium was built,

567
00:33:42,920 --> 00:33:45,960
Royal Stadium in Kansas City.

568
00:33:46,060 --> 00:33:48,060
But advancements in stadium technology

569
00:33:48,160 --> 00:33:50,660
were still being made.

570
00:33:50,760 --> 00:33:55,080
On June 5, 1989, the Toronto Sky Dome opened.

571
00:33:55,180 --> 00:33:57,080
Taking a page from the Astrodome,

572
00:33:57,180 --> 00:34:01,320
the new stadium sported a 282-foot-high dome ceiling

573
00:34:01,420 --> 00:34:02,380
with one improvement.

574
00:34:06,720 --> 00:34:10,220
It was convertible.

575
00:34:10,320 --> 00:34:13,960
Moving at 71 feet per minute, it takes 20 minutes

576
00:34:14,060 --> 00:34:19,260
for the three-piece, 11,000-ton structure to retract.

577
00:34:19,360 --> 00:34:25,580
As the two top pieces telescope backwards,

578
00:34:25,680 --> 00:34:29,280
the end of the dome rotates 180 degrees,

579
00:34:29,380 --> 00:34:33,220
blowing the lid off the 60,000-seat stadium.

580
00:34:33,320 --> 00:34:35,780
Though improvements like retractable roofs

581
00:34:35,781 --> 00:34:38,020
brought some light back to the game.

582
00:34:38,120 --> 00:34:40,580
The larger venue still lacked intimacy.

583
00:34:40,680 --> 00:34:44,180
A growing cry from fans would soon convince owners

584
00:34:44,280 --> 00:34:46,420
that a change was needed.

585
00:34:46,520 --> 00:34:48,520
Fans wanted something new.

586
00:34:48,620 --> 00:34:52,540
What they would get was a piece of history.

587
00:34:52,640 --> 00:34:56,540
Though believed lost, Comiskey Park's original blueprints

588
00:34:56,640 --> 00:34:59,540
were found in an elevator shaft during the park's demolition

589
00:34:59,640 --> 00:35:00,640
in 1991.

590
00:35:00,641 --> 00:35:05,640
1991.

591
00:35:05,740 --> 00:35:08,840
The concrete donut stadiums had dominated baseball

592
00:35:08,940 --> 00:35:11,280
for nearly 30 years.

593
00:35:11,380 --> 00:35:15,580
Many complained that baseball had lost its sense of intimacy.

594
00:35:15,680 --> 00:35:20,060
In 1992, the Baltimore Orioles took a bold step forward

595
00:35:20,160 --> 00:35:23,600
with their creation of Camden Yards.

596
00:35:23,700 --> 00:35:25,400
It was pretty clear from the outset

597
00:35:25,500 --> 00:35:28,440
that the Orioles and the Maryland Stadium Authority

598
00:35:28,441 --> 00:35:32,680
wanted to do something that would set a new standard.

599
00:35:32,780 --> 00:35:36,680
Joe Speer is the chief architect for the Kansas City-based

600
00:35:36,780 --> 00:35:40,480
HOK Sports Facilities Group, the leading sports

601
00:35:40,580 --> 00:35:43,780
architectural firm in America.

602
00:35:43,880 --> 00:35:47,800
HOK was brought in not only to design Camden Yards,

603
00:35:47,900 --> 00:35:50,300
but also advise on its location.

604
00:35:50,400 --> 00:35:54,400
Well, we actually looked in terms of deciding the site

605
00:35:54,500 --> 00:35:55,940
for Camden Yards in Baltimore.

606
00:35:55,941 --> 00:36:00,480
We looked at a couple of dozen sites.

607
00:36:00,580 --> 00:36:03,880
The mayor at that point did a very smart thing.

608
00:36:03,980 --> 00:36:08,380
He asked the public for input as to where the baseball park

609
00:36:08,480 --> 00:36:09,680
should be built.

610
00:36:09,780 --> 00:36:14,200
And I think we got about 27 or 28 different suggestions

611
00:36:14,300 --> 00:36:16,300
about where the park should be.

612
00:36:16,400 --> 00:36:18,960
And we thought, as did the Orioles and the Stadium

613
00:36:19,060 --> 00:36:22,000
Authority, that if the ballpark were downtown,

614
00:36:22,100 --> 00:36:24,140
people would make a day of it.

615
00:36:24,141 --> 00:36:26,940
Built in Baltimore's historic warehouse district,

616
00:36:27,040 --> 00:36:29,240
close to the Baltimore shoreline,

617
00:36:29,340 --> 00:36:32,280
and parallel to the trolley car tracks used by fans

618
00:36:32,380 --> 00:36:36,480
at the turn of the century, Camden Yards is the crown jewel

619
00:36:36,580 --> 00:36:40,080
of baseball's attempt to create and build stadiums

620
00:36:40,180 --> 00:36:43,000
reminiscent of a bygone era.

621
00:36:43,100 --> 00:36:45,600
Well, there's been a real revolution in baseball stadiums

622
00:36:45,700 --> 00:36:46,800
since the early 90s.

623
00:36:46,900 --> 00:36:49,700
There was a rejection of the previous generation's

624
00:36:49,800 --> 00:36:51,800
cookie-cutter, multi-purpose stadiums.

625
00:36:51,801 --> 00:36:54,360
and look back to earlier in the 20th century

626
00:36:54,361 --> 00:36:57,180
when you had baseball-specific facilities

627
00:36:57,181 --> 00:36:58,520
that were tied to neighborhoods,

628
00:36:58,860 --> 00:37:00,980
beginning with Camden Yards in 1992.

629
00:37:01,760 --> 00:37:04,860
Ballparks were designed with brick and steel

630
00:37:04,861 --> 00:37:06,920
and traditional materials that you saw

631
00:37:06,921 --> 00:37:09,740
in places like Ebbets Field and Scheid Park, those classics.

632
00:37:10,100 --> 00:37:12,840
And part of that is creating an atmosphere

633
00:37:12,841 --> 00:37:16,640
and an intimate feeling of you're close to the action of the game.

634
00:37:16,641 --> 00:37:21,840
The stadiums weren't geared to be both baseball and football.

635
00:37:21,940 --> 00:37:23,440
Again, it was baseball specific.

636
00:37:23,540 --> 00:37:26,080
And that's going back to really the roots of the baseball

637
00:37:26,180 --> 00:37:27,880
stadiums in the early part of the century.

638
00:37:31,980 --> 00:37:36,500
Since 1992, 16 teams have followed the lead of Baltimore's

639
00:37:36,600 --> 00:37:41,300
Camden Yards, building intimate parks that look back to the

640
00:37:41,400 --> 00:37:45,140
ballparks of the past, but feature all of the modern

641
00:37:45,141 --> 00:37:47,140
of the modern amenities.

642
00:37:47,240 --> 00:37:50,840
Six more parks are set to open in the next few years.

643
00:37:50,940 --> 00:37:56,180
By 2012, 22 of the 30 MLB teams will have taken part

644
00:37:56,280 --> 00:37:59,280
in this exciting new era of baseball park construction.

645
00:38:05,300 --> 00:38:07,300
The baseball park building boom also

646
00:38:07,400 --> 00:38:10,040
marked baseball's return from the suburbs back

647
00:38:10,140 --> 00:38:11,940
into the heart of the city.

648
00:38:12,040 --> 00:38:15,140
Most of the new parks that have opened in this modern era

649
00:38:15,141 --> 00:38:16,540
are situated downtown.

650
00:38:16,640 --> 00:38:18,480
And that's not by coincidence.

651
00:38:18,580 --> 00:38:22,740
City planners realized that by having a facility that

652
00:38:22,840 --> 00:38:26,560
attracts tens of thousands of people 81 times a year,

653
00:38:26,660 --> 00:38:30,360
that you were able to bring a lot more revenue.

654
00:38:30,460 --> 00:38:32,360
Helping to rejuvenate neighborhoods

655
00:38:32,460 --> 00:38:36,460
that were on the decline, give a real vibrancy to the cities

656
00:38:36,560 --> 00:38:38,660
where there was none before.

657
00:38:38,760 --> 00:38:41,940
This was certainly the case in Denver, one of the first cities

658
00:38:42,040 --> 00:38:44,800
to follow Baltimore's blueprint for success.

659
00:38:44,801 --> 00:38:49,340
Really our challenge was to design a park that fit that community.

660
00:38:49,341 --> 00:38:56,400
The site is adjacent to the lower downtown district, and we thought the real chance for

661
00:38:56,401 --> 00:39:03,320
doing something incredibly meaningful for the city was really what it would do to the

662
00:39:03,321 --> 00:39:09,040
neighborhoods around the ballpark and what it would mean to the lower downtown area.

663
00:39:09,041 --> 00:39:16,280
So that really, I think, is the success story of Coors Field.

664
00:39:16,380 --> 00:39:19,280
The ballpark building boom has been a hit with both the cities

665
00:39:19,380 --> 00:39:20,880
and the fans.

666
00:39:20,980 --> 00:39:24,620
Not surprisingly, there are big plans on tap for the future.

667
00:39:24,720 --> 00:39:28,460
On opening day 2009, New York City baseball fans

668
00:39:28,560 --> 00:39:30,500
will be treated to two new parks.

669
00:39:30,600 --> 00:39:32,400
The new Yankee Stadium and City Field

670
00:39:32,500 --> 00:39:35,540
are coming in 2009.

671
00:39:35,640 --> 00:39:37,560
Yankee Stadium, again, it's tying very closely

672
00:39:37,561 --> 00:39:41,160
the previous ballpark as much as it can to maintain the tradition.

673
00:39:41,161 --> 00:39:44,560
And at City Field, rather than looking back to Shea Stadium,

674
00:39:44,561 --> 00:39:47,560
which when it closes will be about a 40-year-old stadium,

675
00:39:47,561 --> 00:39:49,100
they're looking back to Ebbets Field,

676
00:39:49,101 --> 00:39:51,600
the classic ballpark from Brooklyn.

677
00:39:51,601 --> 00:39:53,720
While Mets and Yankees fans will enjoy parks

678
00:39:53,721 --> 00:39:56,420
that pay homage to their city's hallowed past,

679
00:39:56,421 --> 00:40:00,020
the Oakland A's are teaming up with technology company Cisco

680
00:40:00,021 --> 00:40:02,320
to create a ballpark of the future.

681
00:40:02,321 --> 00:40:05,120
A Cisco field in the Bay Area, the new stadium

682
00:40:05,121 --> 00:40:09,020
for the Oakland A's is really very much a 21st century

683
00:40:09,120 --> 00:40:10,420
example of new ballparks.

684
00:40:10,520 --> 00:40:12,920
We are not a retro ballpark, which

685
00:40:13,020 --> 00:40:16,600
is the traditional ballparks we've seen, some great ones.

686
00:40:16,700 --> 00:40:19,300
We've looked at all of them, and we

687
00:40:19,400 --> 00:40:22,440
feel we're going to do something that is not retro

688
00:40:22,540 --> 00:40:24,240
and not a spaceship either.

689
00:40:28,240 --> 00:40:31,380
Cisco Field is scheduled to open in 2011.

690
00:40:31,480 --> 00:40:33,980
The 32,000 seat park will be one of the smallest

691
00:40:33,981 --> 00:40:37,980
in the majors, but it will also be the most technologically advanced.

692
00:40:37,981 --> 00:40:42,980
It's really, really exciting what the Athletics and Cisco are doing in the development of

693
00:40:42,981 --> 00:40:44,033
the new Cisco field.

694
00:40:44,034 --> 00:40:47,980
What that's going to allow them to do is to have a much more intimate part,

695
00:40:47,981 --> 00:40:51,980
something that is designed strictly for baseball and one that's going to take advantage of

696
00:40:51,981 --> 00:40:56,980
all of the new technologies that are available in sports facilities.

697
00:40:56,981 --> 00:41:01,980
It's interesting trying to find the balance of too much technology that's going to, you know,

698
00:41:01,981 --> 00:41:05,880
take a great game and destroy it.

699
00:41:05,881 --> 00:41:10,120
I think we've always taken the viewpoint that technology can

700
00:41:10,121 --> 00:41:14,120
enhance a great game and make it even better.

701
00:41:14,121 --> 00:41:16,820
What this means is bringing wireless technology to nearly

702
00:41:16,821 --> 00:41:19,440
every aspect of a day at the ballpark.

703
00:41:19,441 --> 00:41:23,240
At Cisco, your Wi-Fi device will play the part of your ticket,

704
00:41:23,241 --> 00:41:26,140
your wallet, and your television set.

705
00:41:26,141 --> 00:41:31,040
Smart devices like cell phones, PDAs, or radio frequency ID cards

706
00:41:31,041 --> 00:41:33,640
will store all your personal information.

707
00:41:33,641 --> 00:41:36,680
These devices will carry your seating information

708
00:41:36,681 --> 00:41:39,880
and store a cash balance that you can use at concession stands

709
00:41:39,881 --> 00:41:40,940
and gift shops.

710
00:41:40,941 --> 00:41:43,820
When you return to your seat, you catch up on the action

711
00:41:43,821 --> 00:41:47,860
by streaming instant replays from a variety of camera angles.

712
00:41:47,861 --> 00:41:50,460
How many times have you been either late to a game

713
00:41:50,461 --> 00:41:53,400
or between innings, and you hear the roar of the crowd

714
00:41:53,401 --> 00:41:55,600
behind you, and there's nothing more frustrating than,

715
00:41:55,601 --> 00:41:56,700
I missed the play.

716
00:42:01,040 --> 00:42:04,040
While this is technology an early 20th century baseball

717
00:42:04,140 --> 00:42:06,780
fan could have never dreamed of, in some ways,

718
00:42:06,880 --> 00:42:09,280
this interactive setting will give modern baseball

719
00:42:09,380 --> 00:42:11,780
a sense of intimacy and accessibility found

720
00:42:11,880 --> 00:42:14,280
in those smaller early parks.

721
00:42:14,380 --> 00:42:17,280
In fact, Cisco Field's luxury suites

722
00:42:17,380 --> 00:42:20,700
will offer the next best thing to a spot in the dugout.

723
00:42:20,800 --> 00:42:23,400
The premium fan experience in the luxury suite

724
00:42:23,500 --> 00:42:26,700
where if they want to see what the coach is seeing,

725
00:42:26,800 --> 00:42:29,800
they have the ability to get that high-definition feed

726
00:42:29,801 --> 00:42:32,800
from the control room right to the luxury suite.

727
00:42:32,801 --> 00:42:34,800
I think it's going to be really exciting to see

728
00:42:34,801 --> 00:42:38,800
how technology is integrated into the new ballpark

729
00:42:38,801 --> 00:42:40,800
in Fremont for the athletics.

730
00:42:40,801 --> 00:42:43,800
If the first couple of decades of the 20th century

731
00:42:43,801 --> 00:42:46,800
was the original classic era of ballpark designs,

732
00:42:46,801 --> 00:42:48,800
I don't think it's far-fetched at all to say

733
00:42:48,801 --> 00:42:53,800
that the second golden era is the era we're in right now.

734
00:42:56,800 --> 00:42:58,800
From the sandlots of the 19th century,

735
00:42:58,801 --> 00:43:02,800
modern technological wonders, baseball parks

736
00:43:02,900 --> 00:43:05,300
are part of our heritage.

737
00:43:05,400 --> 00:43:08,740
With each revered park that comes down,

738
00:43:08,840 --> 00:43:11,040
we lose a little innocence.

739
00:43:11,140 --> 00:43:14,740
But with each new park, we embrace the future.

740
00:43:14,840 --> 00:43:17,160
But no matter how the parks change,

741
00:43:17,260 --> 00:43:20,660
they will always be the homes to America's pastime.

